    "case_id": "247",
    "case_summary": "A 46-year-old Japanese woman with a history of epilepsy presented with redness and swelling of the right breast. On examination, there was erythema and swelling in the lower right breast. Ultrasound revealed a 54 × 41 × 31 mm low-echo mass with partially unclear margins in the outer right breast. Biopsy showed invasive ductal carcinoma (no special type), high grade, ER+, PgR–, HER2 3+, Ki67 70%. Whole-body PET-CT revealed increased uptake in the right axilla, anterior mediastinum, left pleura, and liver, consistent with cT4dN3M1 stage IV breast cancer. She received four cycles of FEC chemotherapy, after which lesions nearly disappeared, and then trastuzumab plus pertuzumab with docetaxel, achieving a clinical complete response maintained for 23 months. Two years after treatment initiation, she developed a persistent headache. CT and MRI of the head revealed a 30-mm mass lesion in the right temporal lobe.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma of the central nervous system\nB. Breast cancer brain metastasis was considered most likely due to the patient’s history and imaging features\nC. Glioblastoma was considered because solitary ring\nD. Primary CNS lymphoma was included in the differential given that ring\nE. Brain abscess was also part of the differential for ring\nF. Metastatic breast cancer was excluded by final immunohistochemical analysis demonstrating lymphoma markers rather than\nG. None\nH. None\n",
